  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Dune stinkhorn is a distinctive fungus characterized by its phallic fruiting body that emerges from a gelatinous 'egg' or universal veil. Despite its unpleasant odor, it plays an important ecological role in sandy environments across New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Phallic fruiting body",
      "description": "Tall, cylindrical stem with a honeycomb-textured cap that ranges from whitish to pinkish, typically 4-8 inches tall",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Foul odor",
      "description": "Emits a strong, unpleasant smell resembling rotting flesh, which attracts flies and other insects for spore dispersal",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous egg stage",
      "description": "Fruiting body emerges from a whitish, jelly-like universal veil or 'egg' that remains at the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in sandy soils, coastal dunes, and disturbed sandy areas throughout New England, often appearing after warm rains.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'stinkhorn' refers to the fungus's strategy of mimicking the smell of carrion to attract flies, which unknowingly carry away spores on their bodies to new locations.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
  "contextualKnowledge": null,
